# 2️⃣1️⃣ Dishonest Seller ⭐

Suppose a seller claims to give:

> 1 kg

but actually gives:

> 800 g.

If he charges the normal price of 1 kg, he makes extra profit.

Assume:

Cost of 1 kg = ₹100.

He gives only 800 g.

Cost of 800 g:

[
100\times\frac{800}{1000}
]

[
=₹80
]

But he charges ₹100.

Profit:

[
100-80=₹20
]

Profit percentage:

[
\frac{20}{80}\times100
]

[
\boxed{25%}
]

🔥 The denominator is the **actual cost of goods given**, ₹80.

---

# 2️⃣2️⃣ Buy One Get One

Suppose:

> Buy 1 shirt, get 1 free.

You receive 2 shirts but pay for 1.

If each shirt costs ₹100:

Total value:

[
200
]

Amount paid:

[
100
]

Discount:

[
100
]

Discount percentage:

[
\frac{100}{200}\times100
]

[
\boxed{50%}
]

---

# 2️⃣3️⃣ Markup + Discount

Suppose:

CP = ₹100

Seller marks it up by 50%.

[
MP=150
]

Then gives 20% discount.

[
SP=150(0.8)
]

[
=120
]

Profit:

[
120-100=20
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{20%\ profit}
]

---

# 🔥 2️⃣4️⃣ Important Formula

If an item is marked up by (M%) and discounted by (D%):

[
\boxed{
SP=CP
\left(1+\frac M{100}\right)
\left(1-\frac D{100}\right)
}
]

Example:

Markup 50%, discount 20%:

[
1.5\times0.8=1.2
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{20%\ profit}
]

---

# 🧮 2️⃣5️⃣ GATE-Style Example

An item costs ₹800.

Seller marks it 25% above CP.

Then gives 10% discount.

### Step 1

[
MP=800(1.25)
]

[
=1000
]

### Step 2

[
SP=1000(0.9)
]

[
=900
]

### Step 3

Profit:

[
900-800=100
]

Profit percentage:

[
\frac{100}{800}\times100
]

[
\boxed{12.5%}
]

